Mild localized allergic reaction to mosquito bite present
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Pruritic pale central wheal, Surrounding erythematous flare, Rapid onset post-bite

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-016
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of intense cutaneous reaction and swelling following an insect bite, requiring assessment to rule out systemic anaphylaxis or evolving cellulitis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted rapid onset of a swollen, erythematous lesion post-insect bite. Urgent physical assessment was strictly required prior to the next in-hours period to evaluate airway patency, confirm hemodynamic stability, and rule out early signs of an impending systemic allergic reaction (anaphylaxis) which could rapidly deteriorate.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I got bitten by something and the bite swelled up instantly and is incredibly itchy.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports the rapid onset of symptoms within minutes of a recognized insect bite occurring outdoors. Describes an intensely pruritic cutaneous lesion at the site of the bite that evolved quickly into an elevated bump. Notes a surrounding well-demarcated erythematous flare. Complete absence of respiratory distress, wheezing, throat tightness, or lip swelling. Denies lightheadedness or generalized hives away from the bite site.
Functional Status: Fully independent, no respiratory compromise.
Social/Safety Concerns: Anxiety regarding potential severe allergic reactions.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 122/76 | HR: 82 | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused, speaking in full sentences without stridor, aggressively scratching the lesion.
Physical Exam: Skin examination reveals a central elevated pale wheal with a surrounding well-demarcated erythematous flare. Diagnostic triad confirmed: pruritic pale central wheal, surrounding erythematous flare, and history of rapid onset post-bite. A central punctum is occasionally visible. Absence of generalized urticaria. Pulmonary exam reveals clear bilateral breath sounds with no wheeze. Normal oropharynx without mucosal edema.
Environmental Observations: Patio door open, high mosquito activity outside.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient took no premedication prior to consultation.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Mild localized allergic reaction to mosquito bite present - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Systemic Anaphylaxis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal blood pressure, absence of airway swelling or stridor, and lack of generalized urticaria.

Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Extremely rapid onset (minutes), absence of deep tissue induration, and lack of localized warmth or fever.

Spider Bite (Necrotic) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of central ischemia, blistering, or severe radiating pain.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient experienced a rapid-onset, expanding cutaneous reaction following an insect bite. Given the potential for insect venom to trigger life-threatening anaphylaxis, the patient required immediate face-to-face evaluation to assess airway and circulatory status. The assessment could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period because anaphylaxis can cause fatal airway obstruction within minutes. Clinical confirmation of a purely localized histamine reaction safely avoided unnecessary emergency department transfer and facilitated appropriate symptomatic management.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Application of topical 1% hydrocortisone cream. Recommendation for an oral non-sedating antihistamine (e.g., cetirizine) to reduce histamine-mediated pruritus.
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply an ice pack wrapped in a cloth to any new bites for 10 minutes immediately after they occur to constrict blood vessels, minimizing venom spread and dramatically reducing the initial histamine spike.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review if the redness expands continuously after 24 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if you develop sudden difficulty breathing, swelling of the face, lips, or tongue, a hoarse voice, or feeling faint.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is a localized allergic response; prior mild reactions do not guarantee future reactions will not be systemic.
Clinical Course: The central wheal will resolve within a few hours, while a firm red, itchy bump may persist for several days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you develop hives all over your body, wheezing, or a sensation that your throat is closing.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The mosquito injected a tiny amount of saliva, and your immune system released histamine to fight it, causing the itch and swelling. Antihistamines block this chemical signal.
Actionable Steps: Take the oral antihistamine daily until the itch stops. Do not scratch the bite, as this breaks the skin and introduces bacteria. Use ice for acute itch relief.

